<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="calcwparammq" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Mini-review of course topics: [PATHS]  Calculus with parametrized curves.</title>

<p>(This is one of the two options that earn the <q>WILD any calculus</q> sticker in the <q>PATHS</q> category.)  <em>The overall idea:  Throughout our Calculus I and Calculus II experience, we have developed ways to discover various geometric aspects of graphs that are expressed in the form <q><m>y</m> as a function of <m>x</m>.</q>  We can discover <em>exactly</em> the same geometric aspects of graphs that are expressed via parametric equations; we just have to adapt our <q><m>xy</m>-notions</q> appropriately.</em></p>

<p>Memory work<mdash/>do this <em>NOW</em>, <em>OUT LOUD</em>:
<ul>
<li>  <em>EXPLAIN</em> the tricks of <q>using <m>\frac{dx}{dt} \cdot dt</m> whenever you need <m>\Delta x</m></q> and <q>using <m>\frac{dy}{dt} \cdot dt</m> whenever you need <m>\Delta y</m>.</q> </li>
<li>  <em>STATE</em> the six formulas given in the supplement. </li>
</ul></p>
<p>For the quiz, be ready to do the following:
<ul>
<li>  <p>Given parametric equations for a curve and a basic sketch of the curve, set up integrals representing the following six quantities <em>in terms of <m>t</m></em>:</p>
<ul>
<li>  <m>\displaystyle{\frac{dy}{dx}}</m> (the slope of the curve's tangent lines); </li>
<li>  the length of the curve; </li>
<li>  the volume of the solid generated by revolving the region under the curve around the <m>x</m>-axis; </li>
<li>  <m>\displaystyle{\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}}</m> (the concavity of the curve); </li>
<li>  the area under the curve; and </li>
<li>  the surface area of the surface generated by revolving the curve around the <m>x</m>-axis.  (This last one will not be <em>REQUIRED</em> until we have seen <q>general</q> surface area in class, but it will always be an <em>OPTION</em>.)  </li>
</ul>
<p>You will not have to find <em>ALL</em> of them on any given quiz; generally you will be asked for <em>TWO</em>.  But be ready to give <em>ANY</em> of them.</p></li>
</ul></p>
<p>Other consideration:
<ul>
<li>    <em>More</em> helpful than simply memorizing the six formulas:  Practice <em>deriving</em> all of them (many with the <q>green common thread</q> in mind)! </li>
</ul></p>

<subsection><title>Examples</title>

<example>
    <p>Consider the curve given by the parametric equations
<md> x = \sin^2t,    y = \sin t \cos t,    0 \leq t \leq \frac{\pi}{2}. </md></p>
<p><alert>Complete BOTH <q>bullets.</q></alert></p>
<ul>
<li>  Find <m>\displaystyle{\frac{dy}{dx}}</m> in terms of <m>t</m>.  Simplify it as far as possible, using algebra and trigonometric identities. </li>
<li>  <p>Give an expression in terms of <m>t</m> for <em>ONE</em> of the following.  Simplify it as far as possible, using algebra and trigonometric identities.  If your choice involves an integral, set up but DO NOT EVALUATE or algebraically simplify the integral.</p>
<ul>
<li>  the length of the curve </li>
<li>  the area under the curve </li>
<li>  the second derivative <m>\displaystyle{\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}}</m> in terms of <m>t</m> </li>
<li>  the volume of the solid generated by revolving the region under the curve around the <m>x</m>-axis </li>
<li>  the surface area of the surface generated by revolving the curve around the <m>x</m>-axis </li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
</example>

<example>
    <p>Consider the curve given by the parametric equations
<md> x = \frac{2}{3}(1-t)^{3/2},   y = \frac{2}{3}(1+t)^{3/2},   -\frac{3}{4} \leq t \leq 0. </md></p>
<p><alert>Complete BOTH <q>bullets.</q></alert></p>
<ul>
<li>  Find <m>\displaystyle{\frac{dy}{dx}}</m> in terms of <m>t</m>.  Simplify it as far as possible, using algebra and trigonometric identities. </li>
<li>  <p>Give an expression in terms of <m>t</m> for <em>ONE</em> of the following.  Simplify it as far as possible, using algebra and trigonometric identities.  If your choice involves an integral, set up but DO NOT EVALUATE or algebraically simplify the integral.</p>
<ul>
<li>  the length of the curve </li>
<li>  the area under the curve </li>
<li>  the second derivative <m>\displaystyle{\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}}</m> in terms of <m>t</m> </li>
<li>  the volume of the solid generated by revolving the region under the curve around the <m>x</m>-axis </li>
<li>  the surface area of the surface generated by revolving the curve around the <m>x</m>-axis </li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>